The completed function is:
fib(n) {
  if (n > M) error();
  if (n == 0) return 1;
  if (n == 1) return 1;
  if (f[n] != 0) return f[n];
  t = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
  f[n] = t;
  return t;
}
Use a local t in each recursive activation. Since both base cases equal1, every valid Fibonacci result is positive. Thus zero safely means “not computed” under the stated exact-arithmetic model. On a cache miss, recursively obtain the two predecessors, store their sum before returning, and reuse it whenever n is requested again. For example fib(4) obtains fib(3)=3, then reads the cached fib(2)=2, producing5. The base cases need not be stored because they already take constant time. With overflowing machine arithmetic, zero might cease to be a valid sentinel; practical implementations can use an independent computed flag or arbitrary-precision values.

The complete intended protocol is:
Reader() {
L1: wait(mutex);
  if (W == 0) {
    R = R + 1;
    signal(mutex);                 // box 1
  } else {
    signal(mutex);                 // box 2
    goto L1;
  }
  /* read shared data */
  wait(mutex);
  R = R - 1;
  signal(mutex);
}
Writer() {
L2: wait(mutex);
  if (R > 0 || W != 0) {           // box 3
    signal(mutex);
    goto L2;
  }
  W = 1;
  signal(mutex);
  /* write shared data */
  wait(mutex);
  W = 0;
  signal(mutex);
}
wait/signal must implement a genuine atomic binary semaphore with appropriate memory visibility. Merely reading and writing an ordinary flag is not a substitute. All tests and updates of R,W occur under mutex, so checking admission and registering the admitted reader/writer are atomic relative to competitors. A writer enters only when no reader and no writer is active, and W=1 prevents later admissions until it finishes. Multiple readers are allowed while W=0. Both reader branches must release mutex; retaining it would block exits or retries and can deadlock. The mutex protects admission bookkeeping, not the whole read/write operation; R and W reserve access while mutex is released. Safety holds, but fairness is not guaranteed.

The null-tree base case fixes depth as the number of nodes on a longest root-to-leaf path. Each nonempty tree contributes its root plus the larger child depth. Full compilable function:

#include <stddef.h>
typedef struct node {
    int key;
    struct node *left, *right;
} *Tree;
int depth(Tree t) {
    int x, y;
    if (t == NULL) return 0;
    x = depth(t->left);
    y = depth(t->right); /* S1 */
    if (x > y) return x + 1; /* S2 */
    else return y + 1; /* S3 */
}

A leaf returns 1. The preceding tree returns 4. Each node is visited once, giving O(n) time and O(h) recursive stack space; an edge-count definition would require a different base case and is not the convention of the supplied program.

1. Infer the parameter roles from the printed central action. move(n,A,B,C) prints a move of disk n from A to C, so A is the source, C the destination, and B the spare pole. A recursive call must preserve this source/spare/destination order even when the pole names are permuted.

2. Fill the stopping condition. For zero disks there is nothing to move. Therefore execute the body only when n > 0. Using n > 1 would incorrectly skip the required single-disk move.

3. Clear the largest disk. Before disk n can move from A to C, the n−1 disks above it must move from A to B with C available as spare. In the function’s argument order this is move(n−1,A,C,B).

4. Move the largest disk, then restore the smaller tower above it. The printed action moves disk n from A to C. Next transfer the n−1 disks from B to C using A as spare, which is move(n−1,B,A,C).

5. The completed valid C function is:

```c
void move(int n, char A, char B, char C)
